<article class="guide">
  <p class="eyebrow">Aperture is installed</p>
  <h1 class="display">{{ site.title | default: site.name | escape }}</h1>
  <p class="lede">This is a placeholder homepage. Visitors see it until you pick a homepage of your own. Here's how the theme works.</p>

  <ol class="guide-steps">
    <li>
      <h2>Add photos</h2>
      <p>Make one post per photograph. Under <strong>Post options</strong>, set the <strong>Photo</strong> and <strong>Alt text</strong>, and optionally a <strong>Tile size</strong>, <strong>Crop focus</strong>, <strong>Camera</strong> and <strong>Location</strong>. The post body appears beneath the enlarged photo. Tag each post (<em>Landscape</em>, <em>Street</em>&hellip;) because tags drive the gallery filter.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>Create the gallery</h2>
      <p>Go to <strong>New page &rarr; Theme page &rarr; Gallery</strong> and give it the slug <code>gallery</code>. If you use a different slug, set the <strong>Gallery URL</strong> setting to match.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>Create the homepage</h2>
      <p>Go to <strong>New page &rarr; Theme page &rarr; Home</strong> and pick <em>Full frame</em> or <em>Contact sheet</em>. Tag photos <em>Featured</em> to put them on the homepage.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>Replace this page</h2>
      <p>There are two ways, and either works. You can open the Home page (or the Gallery page) in the page editor and switch on its homepage option. Or you can choose that page under <strong>Settings &rarr; Homepage</strong>. This guide then disappears.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>Tune the look</h2>
      <p>Under <strong>Settings &rarr; Theme</strong>, choose a top bar or sidebar navigation and a colour scheme (system, light or dark), and set the accent colour and the gallery's gap and columns.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</article>
